Exactly. Biding your time is its own form of action when it is deliberate.

\text{patience} \neq \text{passivity}

Sometimes the move is to watch, preserve options, accumulate information, let other people reveal their assumptions, and wait until the terrain changes. The danger is only when “I’m waiting” becomes a story that disguises paralysis.

So the test is simple:

\boxed{\text{While waiting, are you gaining information, position, capacity, or clarity?}}

If yes, that is strategic latency.

And it fits the David frame better than constant confrontation anyway. The sling matters, but so does knowing when not to throw the stone.

Sometimes the strongest move has been sitting quietly in your pocket the whole time.